55,3,1,talk,Servas,,"We have our candidates. Microbiome Atlas (A) involves field samples, Plant Drought (B) uses greenhouses, Protein Folding (C) uses spectroscopy and simulation, and Amphibians (D) uses live colonies.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,1,talk,Midori,,"Looking at these, the Microbiome Atlas seems like the catalogue-style project I found to be so durable. But @Toshio, wouldn't the field samples be risky?","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@Servas, the Plant Drought project is a no-go! The glasshouses are booked solid and we have no technicians left to help!","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Midori, the Atlas is indeed a catalogue, but it depends on field sites which Toshio found to be unstable. Protein Folding (C) combines simulation—which is stable—with spectroscopy.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Midori"",""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, the spectroscopy suite is a liability, as Kenji noted. However, Amphibians (D) is the worst choice; live colonies are the primary source of schedule overruns.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,2,talk,Servas,,"@Benjamin, you mentioned simulation. My data shows we have plenty of applicants for modelling and code posts. Protein Folding (C) leverages this strength.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,Midori,,But @Kenji mentioned the spectroscopy suite was idle for eleven weeks. Would that paralyze the Protein Folding project?,"{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Kenji,,It might! But the Atlas (A) needs field sites that are burning or flooding. That sounds even scarier than a broken machine!,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Midori, a machine can be repaired or a different suite used. A flooded field site is a total loss of a season. The Atlas is too risky.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"I agree. The Atlas (A) and Amphibians (D) are too dependent on volatile biological systems. Between B and C, B is impossible due to glasshouse capacity.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,3,talk,Servas,,It seems we have reached a logical conclusion. Protein Folding (C) utilizes the available modelling workforce and avoids the most severe environmental risks.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,Midori,,"I am slightly worried about the spectroscopy, but compared to the other options, it seems the most balanced. I can support Protein Folding.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Kenji,,I'm on board! It's better than trying to squeeze into a full greenhouse or waiting for a flood to go away. I vote for Protein Folding!,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,The logic is inescapable. Protein Folding (C) minimizes the risk of schedule overruns by leaning on computation and avoiding the breeding colony trap. I vote for C.,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,3,talk,Toshio,,I concur. It is the only candidate that doesn't rely on the variables that have historically caused our picks to stall. I vote for Protein Folding.,"{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,4,talk,Servas,,I agree. It aligns with the available human capital and avoids the most critical bottlenecks. I vote for Protein Folding.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,4,talk,Midori,,I also vote for Protein Folding. It is the most pragmatic choice given the constraints we've uncovered.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
